    "titleMy": "日本の街で見かける安全標識とルール (Safety Signs and Rules in Japanese Streets) (ဖတ်ရှုခြင်း)",
    "titleMyanmar": "日本の街で見かける安全標識とルール (Safety Signs and Rules in Japanese Streets) (ဖတ်ရှုခြင်း)",
    "translationMy": "When walking in Japanese towns, one notices many signs and notices on utility poles, roads, and building walls. These signs are installed to protect people's safety and prevent accidents.\n\nFor example, at construction sites, \"Keep Out\" (立入禁止) is written in large characters. This means \"Because it is dangerous, you must not enter inside.\" Also, yellow lines are drawn on train station platforms, and announcements say \"Step back inside the line.\" Because it is dangerous when trains approach, you must not step outside the yellow line.\n\nOn roads, there are inverted triangular red signs saying \"STOP\" (止まれ). People driving cars or bicycles must always come to a complete stop at the spot of this sign. Pedestrians should also not rush to cross roads, but follow traffic lights and walk safely. Let us all understand sign meanings correctly and follow the rules together."
