  "reading": {
    "id": "n4.l05.r01",
    "lessonId": "n4.l05",
    "title": "初めての温泉旅行アドバイス (Advice for Your First Hot Spring Trip)",
    "level": "N4",
    "text": "冬の日本を旅するなら、雪景色を眺めながら入る露天風呂は格別の体験です。多くの留学生や外国人観光客が「温泉に行ってみたいけれど、どこがいいか分からない」と相談に来ます。\n\n東京から近くて気軽に行ける場所を探しているなら、神奈川県の箱根温泉が一番おすすめです。新宿駅から特急ロマンスカーに乗れば、一時間半足らずで到着します。温泉街には歴史のある旅館が多く、おいしい日本料理や伝統的なおもてなしを堪能できます。\n\nもし泊まりがけでゆっくり温泉を楽しむなら、浴衣の着方や温泉の正しいマナーを事前に確認しておくと安心です。湯船に入る前に必ず体を洗うことや、湯船の中にタオルを入れないことなど、簡単なルールを守れば誰でも気持ちよく楽しめます。温泉旅行の計画なら、地元の観光協会のウェブサイトをチェックすることをおすすめします。",
    "kanaText": "冬の日本を旅するなら、雪景色を眺めながら入る露天風呂は格別の体験です。多くの留学生や外国人観光客が「温泉に行ってみたいけれど、どこがいいか分からない」と相談に来ます。\n\n東京から近くて気軽に行ける場所を探しているなら、神奈川県の箱根温泉が一番おすすめです。新宿駅から特急ロマンスカーに乗れば、一時間半足らずで到着します。温泉街には歴史のある旅館が多く、おいしい日本料理や伝統的なおもてなしを堪能できます。\n\nもし泊まりがけでゆっくり温泉を楽しむなら、浴衣の着方や温泉の正しいマナーを事前に確認しておくと安心です。湯船に入る前に必ず体を洗うことや、湯船の中にタオルを入れないことなど、簡単なルールを守れば誰でも気持ちよく楽しめます。温泉旅行の計画なら、地元の観光協会のウェブサイトをチェックすることをおすすめします。",
    "translation": "If you travel in winter Japan, taking an open-air bath while gazing at snowy scenery is an exceptional experience. Many international students and foreign tourists consult saying, \"I want to visit a hot spring, but I don't know where is good.\"\n\nIf you are searching for a place close to Tokyo that you can visit casually, Hakone Onsen in Kanagawa Prefecture is my top recommendation. If you take the Limited Express Romancecar from Shinjuku Station, you arrive in less than an hour and a half. The hot spring town has many historic inns where you can savor delicious Japanese cuisine and traditional hospitality.\n\nIf you are staying overnight to enjoy hot springs leisurely, checking how to wear yukata and proper onsen etiquette in advance will give you peace of mind. By following simple rules like washing your body before getting into the bathtub and not putting towels into the bathwater, anyone can enjoy themselves pleasantly. If it is planning an onsen trip, I recommend checking the local tourism association website.",
    "questionIds": [
      "n4.l05.q021",
      "n4.l05.q022",
      "n4.l05.q023",
      "n4.l05.q024",
      "n4.l05.q025"
    ],
    "difficulty": 2,
    "glossary": [],
    "prov": {
      "source": "Nihongo N2 Master Editorial Team (Native Review)",
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "status": "reviewed",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-01T00:00:00Z",
      "rev": 2
    },
    "titleMy": "初めての温泉旅行アドバイス (Advice for Your First Hot Spring Trip) (ဖတ်ရှုခြင်း)",
    "titleMyanmar": "初めての温泉旅行アドバイス (Advice for Your First Hot Spring Trip) (ဖတ်ရှုခြင်း)",
    "translationMy": "If you travel in winter Japan, taking an open-air bath while gazing at snowy scenery is an exceptional experience. Many international students and foreign tourists consult saying, \"I want to visit a hot spring, but I don't know where is good.\"\n\nIf you are searching for a place close to Tokyo that you can visit casually, Hakone Onsen in Kanagawa Prefecture is my top recommendation. If you take the Limited Express Romancecar from Shinjuku Station, you arrive in less than an hour and a half. The hot spring town has many historic inns where you can savor delicious Japanese cuisine and traditional hospitality.\n\nIf you are staying overnight to enjoy hot springs leisurely, checking how to wear yukata and proper onsen etiquette in advance will give you peace of mind. By following simple rules like washing your body before getting into the bathtub and not putting towels into the bathwater, anyone can enjoy themselves pleasantly. If it is planning an onsen trip, I recommend checking the local tourism association website."
