      "status": "reviewed",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-01T00:00:00Z",
      "rev": 2
    },
    "titleMy": "日本の食品サンプル技術と海外の反応 (ဖတ်ရှုခြင်း)",
    "titleMyanmar": "日本の食品サンプル技術と海外の反応 (ဖတ်ရှုခြင်း)",
    "translationMy": "At the entrances of restaurants and coffee shops in Japan, one often sees realistic food models displayed inside glass showcases. Called \"food samples\" (shokuhin sanpuru), they are praised internationally as a unique Japanese craft culture.\n\nViewed from a distance, they look as if warm steam is rising or sizzling steak juices are dripping. Foreign tourists visiting Japan for the first time are amazed, exclaiming \"It looks just as though real dishes are laid out!\", and one can see them instinctively snapping photos with smartphones.\n\nAccording to artisans, when crafting food samples, they make meticulous efforts to faithfully reproduce the appetizing coloration and soft textures of real food. For example, the curling of cabbage leaves and the clear color of ramen soup are crafted through close observation of fine details.\n\nEven for foreigners who cannot read Japanese, instead of deciphering menu text, just looking at models resembling actual food reveals portions and ingredients at a glance. Food samples can be called a symbol of Japan's warm hospitality, welcoming guests just like a kind guide."
