  "grammars": [
    {
      "id": "n4.l23.g001",
      "lessonId": "n4.l23",
      "pattern": "[Noun / Na-adj stem / Verb plain] + みたいだ (口語の推量)",
      "meaning": "Colloquial deduction / sensory inference (\"It seems like / looks like / sounds like\")",
      "formation": "Noun + みたいだ (*no の: 子どもみたいだ, 夢みたいだ)\nNa-adj stem + みたいだ (*no な/だ: 静かみたいだ, 暇みたいだ)\nVerb/i-adj (Plain form) + みたいだ (行くみたいだ, 寒かったみたいだ)",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "The colloquial conversational counterpart to 〜ようだ. Expresses an inference or observation in informal spoken Japanese. Unlike 〜ようだ, nouns and na-adjectives attach directly WITHOUT の or な (子どもみたいだ, NOT *子どものみたいだ).",
        "my": "စကားပြော (口語) တွင် အသုံးများသော「〜ようだ」၏ ပေါ့ပေါ့ပါးပါး စကားပြောပုံစံ ဖြစ်သည်။ နာမ်နှင့် na-adjective များတွင်「の」သို့မဟုတ်「な」မထည့်ဘဲ တိုက်ရိုက်ဆက်ရသည် (ဥပမာ- 子どもみたいだ)။"
      },
      "examples": [
        {
          "sentenceId": "n4.l23.s001",
          "note": "Winning the championship feels like a dream."
        },
        {
          "sentenceId": "n4.l23.s008",
          "note": "It seems what Tanaka said was a joke."
