      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "status": "reviewed",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-01T00:00:00Z",
      "rev": 2
    },
    "titleMy": "日本での上手なお願いの仕方 (ဖတ်ရှုခြင်း)",
    "titleMyanmar": "日本での上手なお願いの仕方 (ဖတ်ရှုခြင်း)",
    "translationMy": "When asking someone for a favor in Japanese, it is extremely important to consider the relationship and seniority relative to the person and choose the appropriate expression.\n\nFor example, when asking close friends or colleagues for help, friendly request expressions such as \"Could you help me photocopy these documents?\" or \"Could you lend a hand for a moment?\" are preferred.\n\nHowever, when making a request to university professors, superiors at work, or seniors you meet for the first time, using \"〜te kudasai\" risks giving a slightly demanding and unilateral impression. When requesting superiors, it is proper social etiquette to use polite inquiring expressions that show concern for the person's schedule, such as \"I apologize for troubling you when you are busy, but would you kindly give me guidance?\" or \"Would you be able to write a recommendation letter for me?\"\n\nAdditionally, so that it does not burden the other person even if they decline the request, adding cushion words as a preface, like \"if it is possible\" or \"if your schedule permits,\" enables much smoother communication."
