      "pattern": "Complex Sentence Integration: Giving/Receiving, Nominalization & Embedded Questions",
      "meaning": "Multi-Clause Synthetic Syntax: Directionality of Benefit, Nominalization, and Embedded Clauses",
      "formation": "1. Giving/Receiving: 〜てあげる (speaker gives to other) vs 〜てくれる (other gives to speaker/in-group) vs 〜てもらう (speaker receives assistance from other)\n2. Nominalization: の (sensory perception, stopping, waiting: 見るの, 待つの) vs こと (habits, rules, abilities, speech content: 話すこと, 趣味は〜こと)\n3. Embedded Clauses: [Interrogative + Plain form] + か / [Plain form (*no だ)] + かどうか",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "Consolidates multi-clause syntactic patterns in practical contexts:\n- 〜てあげる / 〜てくれる / 〜てもらう: Directionality is strictly defined by speaker in-group perspective. A friend helping me is 友達が手伝ってくれた; receiving help is 友達に手伝ってもらった.\n- Nominalization の vs こと: Direct sensory perception (見る, 聞く) and physical actions (待つ, 止める) require の. Fixed conceptual predicates (趣味は〜こと, 〜ことができる, 〜ことがある) require こと.\n- Embedded Questions: かどうか for yes/no clauses; か for interrogative clauses with 何/誰/どこ/いつ. Crucially, nouns and na-adjectives drop だ before か / かどうか.",
